## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` (shallow equality) to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects, arrays, and functions compare by reference.

```jsx
// New object reference created every render — child always re-renders
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// New function reference created every render — same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every render of the parent creates a *new* reference, even if the value is identical, so the child sees a "changed" prop.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — memoizes a component; skips re-render if props haven't changed (by reference).

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, config }) => { ... });
```

No point using `React.memo` without stabilizing the props you pass to it.

---

**`useCallback`** — stabilizes a function reference across renders.

```jsx
// Without: new reference every render
const handleClick = () => doSomething(id);

// With: same reference unless `id` changes
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

Use when passing callbacks to `React.memo` children or as `useEffect` dependencies.

---

**`useMemo`** — stabilizes an object/array reference, or skips an expensive computation.

```jsx
// Without: new object every render
const config = { theme, fontSize };

// With: same reference unless dependencies change
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme, fontSize }), [theme, fontSize]);
```

Use for derived objects/arrays passed as props, or genuinely expensive calculations. Don't use it for cheap computations — the bookkeeping costs more than the re-render.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context *value* changes by reference. The common mistake:

```jsx
// Bad: new object literal every render — all consumers re-render constantly
<UserContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>
```

Fix by memoizing the value:

```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<UserContext.Provider value={value}>
```

Also: split large contexts into smaller ones so consumers only subscribe to what they need.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders on every `App` render even when data hasn't changed:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filters = { active: true };                    // new ref each render
  const handleSelect = (id) => setSelected(id);        // new ref each render

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+</button>
      <List filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function List({ filters, onSelect }) { ... }           // no memo
```

**After** — `List` only re-renders when its actual inputs change:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ active: true }), []);       // stable ref
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => setSelected(id), []); // stable ref

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+</button>
      <List filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(function List({ filters, onSelect }) { ... });
```

---

**Rule of thumb:** reach for these tools when you can *measure* a problem (React DevTools Profiler), not preemptively. Premature memoization adds complexity without benefit — the dependency arrays themselves become a source of bugs.
